description:
metadata.rs:341-349, 400-408, 450-458 — three of the four match arms in the create/update/destroy loops follow this pattern:

    Err(BackendSetError::SetError(set_err)) => { ... }
    Err(BackendSetError::Other(e)) => { ... }
    Err(_) => {
        not_created.insert(create_id, json!({
            "type": "serverFail",
            "description": "unhandled backend error variant",
        }));
    }

BackendSetError is #[non_exhaustive] (in jmap-server). The Err(_) catch-all is the contract — a future variant being added in jmap-server should not break compilation in this crate, and at runtime the catch-all routes the new variant to serverFail.

Two issues:
1. The literal string 'unhandled backend error variant' leaks into wire responses if jmap-server ever adds a variant the metadata-server doesn't handle. That's not a great message for a client to receive — it tells the client nothing about what happened and tells the operator nothing about which variant fired.
2. The pattern is copy-pasted three times. Future variant additions require three edits. Three is the threshold where DRY pays.

Suggested rewrite — extract a helper in helpers.rs that consumes BackendSetError<E> and produces a Value:

    pub(crate) fn backend_set_error_to_value<E: std::fmt::Display>(
        e: &BackendSetError<E>,
    ) -> Value {
        match e {
            BackendSetError::SetError(se) => set_error_value(se),
            BackendSetError::Other(inner) => json!({
                "type": "serverFail",
                "description": inner.to_string(),
            }),
            _ => json!({
                "type": "serverFail",
                "description": "unhandled backend error variant",
            }),
        }
    }

Then the three loops collapse to:
    Err(e) => { not_*.insert(id, backend_set_error_to_value(&e)); }

Cost of leaving: 3-way drift risk on future BackendSetError extension. A new variant added to jmap-server's BackendSetError will be silently routed to 'unhandled backend error variant' in metadata-server's wire responses until someone updates the three sites.

This is exactly the bug class the workspace's canonical-template propagation rule is supposed to catch. Worth checking if jmap-mail-server has the same pattern and whether this should be a canonical sweep.

Severity: P3, elegance + future drift hedge.
